    backstories: [
      "They grew up in a lighthouse at the edge of the storm-coast, raised by a grandmother who taught them to read weather like a language. When the maps of their homeland began changing overnight, they left to find out why—and discovered they were one of the last people who could still see the old roads. The road has been their only teacher since.\n\nThey learned early that mercy costs something, but they've never quite worked out what. Every stranger they've helped has left them with a small, unpayable debt, and they carry the ledger of it in their coat pocket, hoping to one day close the account.\n\nWhat they haven't told anyone: the maps didn't start changing until the night they left home.",

      "The court sheltered them until it didn't. They were raised in the healer's tower, taught to close wounds with humming and to walk without sound. When their teacher was accused of a crime they knew she didn't commit, they were the one who let her escape—and the one who took the blame quietly.\n\nNow they travel with a false name and a real gift, sleeping in barns and mending farmers' children in exchange for bread. Every so often, a rider on a black horse passes through the towns they leave, asking questions in a soft voice.\n\nThe healer's last lesson: sometimes the safest place is the one nobody would look.",

      "They were born in a village that sat quietly at the mouth of an enchanted forest. Every generation, the forest asked for one child in exchange for its silence. Their older brother went in and never came out. When it was almost their turn, they went in on purpose—but they came back.\n\nThey remember nothing of what happened inside. They remember everything of what has happened since: the way animals turn their heads when they pass, the way certain doors won't stay shut around them, the way their reflection sometimes lags behind them by a heartbeat.\n\nThey are trying to find their brother, or forget him. They have not decided which.",

      "They are the last apprentice of an order that no longer exists. Their master died before finishing their training, leaving them with half of a ceremony, an incomplete sigil, and a promise they don't fully understand. They wander because staying still feels like admitting the order is really gone.\n\nEvery few months they meet someone else who was also almost-trained by someone almost-forgotten. They exchange fragments, compare scars, and part without ever using each other's real names.\n\nThey are quietly, patiently, assembling a rulebook from pieces. When it is finished, they don't know what they will do with it.",

      "They stole something that wasn't theirs and gave it to someone who needed it more. It was the first honest thing they ever did, and it made them a fugitive. They've been running long enough now that the running feels like home, and the returning to a settled life feels like the impossible thing.\n\nThe object they stole was small: a ring, a key, a page. But the person they gave it to changed the world with it, and now that person is famous, and they are still nobody, and they prefer it that way.\n\nOnly one person knows the truth of what they did. That person has been looking for them for years, not to arrest them, but to thank them.",

      "They were raised by traveling players and grew up learning that every story had at least three versions. They played children in tragedies, ghosts in comedies, and messengers in every play their troupe performed. When the troupe was disbanded—their leader vanished under strange circumstances—they kept the trunk of costumes and set out to find her.\n\nThey introduce themselves differently in every town. They have not lied about their name in seven years, only chosen from the several they truly own. Each of them is a piece of a person who was pieced together in the first place.\n\nThey suspect their troupe leader is not lost. They suspect she is hiding, and that the play she's writing next has a part in it for them.",

      "They were the youngest keeper at a very small temple, tending to a god that nobody visits anymore. They swept the courtyard, lit the candles, and had long conversations with the empty altar out of habit. One morning, the altar answered.\n\nThey have not told anyone. They are not sure if the god was ever really silent, or if they were the first person to be quiet enough to hear it. Either way, the god is lonely, and increasingly asking for things—small favors, tiny errands, a message delivered here or there.\n\nThey are learning that being chosen by a forgotten thing is not the same as being blessed, but it is a kind of company.",

      "They were the child in the village who noticed things—the neighbor's limp, the storm gathering three days out, the way certain guests had the wrong shadow. Their mother told them to keep quiet about it. Their grandmother told them to write it all down. They chose the grandmother.\n\nNow they keep a book, and the book is heavy, and the book has started making requests of its own. Names to underline. Places to visit. Warnings to write in the margins in a hand that isn't quite theirs.\n\nThey are trying to understand whether they are the author of the book or one of its characters."
    ],

